title: Cross-machine sync
tags: Sync
kind: guide
aliases: borrow borrowing give-back
summary: To sync with a repository of another machine, mount its files, borrow it — its .metafolder copied here and loaded, the remote locked — sync, then give it back.

Every sync primitive needs both repositories loaded in one daemon
([[Sync endpoints]]), and a store is refused on a network filesystem
([[Storage safety]]): a repository mounted from another machine cannot be loaded
where it is. It is ''borrowed'' instead: its `.metafolder/` copied to this machine,
the copy loaded and synced like any repository, then sent back.

!! Doing it

```
sshfs other:/data/photos /mnt/other-photos          # the remote's files, mounted
mf repo borrow /mnt/other-photos --to ~/borrowed/photos --name photos@other
mf sync plan photos photos@other … ; mf sync run …   # as with any pair ([[Syncing two repositories]])
mf -n photos@other repo give-back
```

* `mf repo borrow ROOT --to DIR` takes the remote's root as mounted here, and `--metafolder` too when its `.metafolder/` is not inside it. `--to` must be a new directory, on a local disk. It prints the repository's uuid.
* The copy is an external `.metafolder/` ([[Network and special filesystems]]) whose root is the mount: every file the sync reads or writes on that side goes through the mount, and a file it overwrites goes to the copy's trash — across the network, and back with the copy.
* `--name` loads it under another name: names are unique among loaded repositories, and a repository synced across machines often bears the same on both.
* `mf repo give-back` (on the copy) unloads it, checks its store, sends it home and deletes it. It prints the remote's `.metafolder/`.

!! What travels

The borrow copies `config.json`, `config/` and `internal/` but for its backups, the
stores a restore set aside and its sync plans (`backups/`, `pre-restore-*`, `plan-*`),
which stay at home, and the lock files of each store. The copy writes its own `config.json`: the remote's, its root
the mount, renamed when asked, and `borrowed` naming the remote `.metafolder/`
([[Repository layout on disk]]).

The give-back sends ''`internal/` alone'' — the store, the pair stores the repository
hosts ([[The sync database]]), the trash, the slow log —, never `config.json` nor
`config/`: the remote keeps its own name, root and configuration. Each entry is written
beside the remote's under a temporary name (`.returning`), then swapped in; an entry the
copy no longer has is removed. A sync plan the copy hosts ([[The sync plan]]) is a
repository loaded from inside it: the give-back unloads it, and does not send it — the
next `mf sync plan` makes a new one. The mark goes last, so an interrupted give-back leaves
the remote still marked and the copy still there: giving back again completes it.

The copy never tracks the remote's `internal/`, although it sits inside the root the
copy watches: it is excluded like the copy's own (`RepoState::untracked`).

!! The lock

While borrowed, the remote holds `internal/borrowed.json` — who borrowed it, when, into
which copy —, and its own daemon refuses to load it (`409`), saying so. Nothing may write
to the remote store meanwhile, or the give-back would lose it.

A daemon's `flock` on a store ([[The repository lock]]) cannot be seen across a network
mount, so a daemon that holds a store also says so in `internal/kv/daemon.holder` — its
host, pid and since when —, removed when it lets go. A borrow refuses a repository whose
holder file is there: unload it on its machine first. A crash leaves the file behind;
`--force` borrows past it, once you know that daemon is gone.

The borrow writes its mark, then reads the holder; a load takes the lock, writes the
holder, then reads the mark: of a borrow and a load that race, one sees the other. That
holds as far as the mount shows each write to the other side at once — an `sshfs` mount
caches attributes for a few seconds, so do not borrow a repository being loaded there.

The give-back is refused (`409`) when the remote's mark no longer names this copy: the
borrow was broken there, and the remote may have been written since. The copy stays
loaded.

!! When it goes wrong

* ''The copy is lost'' (its disk, a deletion): the remote stays marked. Delete `internal/borrowed.json` on the remote by hand — the refusal names it —, and what was written in the copy is lost with it. Nothing else changed on the remote: its store is as it was when borrowed.
* ''The remote's files changed meanwhile'' on its own machine: its daemon was not watching them. Run `mf reconcile` there after the give-back, as after any time unwatched ([[Freezing metadata by not watching]]).
* ''The copy no longer checks clean'': the give-back sends nothing. Load it, `mf repo reindex` it, and give it back again ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).
